## 13. Interview Preparation

**Q: "Tell me about your Aegis Nexus AI project." (2-minute explanation)**
"I built a real-time autonomous voice AI system designed for enterprise security teams. The core problem it solves is the latency in incident response. When a server breaches at 3 AM, human intervention takes too long. I built a Node.js orchestrator that connects directly to Twilio via WebSockets to stream raw telephone audio. I pipe that audio into Speechmatics for real-time transcription, feed it to Google Gemini which acts as the reasoning engine and has access to execute server-side functions, and then synthesize the response using ElevenLabs. The hardest part was building the custom audio buffering and 'barge-in' logic so the AI can handle being interrupted naturally, just like a human."

**Deep Technical Question:** *"How did you handle the latency between the LLM generating text and the TTS generating audio?"*
**Answer:** "I heavily optimized the pipeline using streaming. Instead of waiting for Gemini to generate the full paragraph, I stream the tokens chunk-by-chunk. As soon as a logical sentence or phrase is formed, I pipe that text directly into ElevenLabs. ElevenLabs then streams the MP3 bytes back, which my Node server transcodes on-the-fly into µ-law format and pushes down the Twilio WebSocket. This parallel streaming architecture brings the time-to-first-byte latency down to under 800ms."

**HR Version:** "I built an AI system that acts as an automated customer service rep over the phone, but it's fast enough and smart enough to handle complex IT security problems."

